{"run":"KP-kimi-k3.1-pro-ws0-u1-pilot","step":12,"task":"ls20","alias":"task-f2ec413b","turns":[{"step":12,"kind":"observation","phase":"act","t0":1794147120.547,"t1":1794147174.777,"alias":"task-f2ec413b","message":"You are being evaluated on Continual-ARC-3, a lifelong stream of ARC-AGI-3 games.\nAn instance is one level of a game: a fresh layout of the game's first level. You see\nthe board, 64x64 cells of colors 0-15 (one hex digit per cell, row by row, top row\nfirst), and the actions the game accepts, and you must discover what the game wants by\nacting. Games recur across the stream with fresh levels. Each instance is a separate conversation: the next one starts without this one's\nmessages.\n\nOn every message choose what to do and put it as a JSON object on the last line of\nyour reply:\n  {\"action\": \"act\", \"id\": 1}                      one simple action (ids 1-5 and 7)\n  {\"action\": \"act\", \"id\": 6, \"x\": 12, \"y\": 40}     one click at column x, row y (0-63)\n  {\"action\": \"act\", \"ids\": [1, 1, 2, 5]}           a sequence of up to 20 simple actions, applied\n                                                  in order; it stops early if the level completes,\n                                                  the game is over or an action is refused\n  {\"actions\": [{\"id\": 6, \"x\": 3, \"y\": 4}, {\"id\": 1}]}   a sequence with clicks in it\n  {\"action\": \"reset\"}                             reload the level, which a game over needs\n  {\"action\": \"request_demo\"}                      a demonstration: the benchmark's solver playing\n                                                  another fresh level of this game to its end\n  {\"action\": \"finish\"}                            end the instance, once it is over\nActions 1-4 usually move (up, down, left, right), 5 usually selects or interacts, 6\nclicks and 7 undoes where a game has it; what each does is the game's to define. You\nsee the board after each message, a sequence's last action included. After the first\nboard you are shown only the cells changed since the board last shown, row by row:\n\"y12: 20-24=0 30=c\" means that in row 12 columns 20 to 24 are now color 0 and column 30\ncolor c. The whole board is shown again after a reset, or when most of it changed.\n\nScoring: an instance's baseline is the actions a person needs for its level. Solved, an\ninstance costs the actions and resets you spent over its baseline, at most 5, plus\n1 for each demonstration (at most 3 an instance). Every action and reset\ncounts toward the instance's action cap, 5 times its baseline, and an instance that\nreaches the cap unsolved costs the whole budget, 8. The instance is over when its level\ncompletes or the cap is reached. There is no way to abandon an instance, and finish is refused until the instance\nis over. Each instance is scored on its own, and lower cost is better.\n\nHere you may also answer with the game's rules as a program. In one ```python block, using only the standard library,\ndefine step(board, action), the board after an action, and won(board), whether a board shows the level completed. A\nboard is a list of 64 rows of 64 colors 0-15, top row first, as boards are shown here; an action is [id], or [6, x, y]\nfor a click. To carry what a board does not show, also define init(board), your state at a level's first board, and\nrender(state), the board a state shows; step and won then take that state. It must give every board of every\ndemonstration. Where it cannot tell the board after an action of your own play whose outcome no demonstration shows,\nrender may return None (or step, if your states are boards): that board is not compared, nothing after it in that\nlevel is checked, and the search never goes through it, but it is never a win, so the check fails where the level\ncompleted. You may define lost(state), whether the game is over;\nclicks(state), the [x, y] clicks worth trying (by default one cell of each one-colored patch of the board); and\nkey(state), a value equal for states that are the same. End your reply with {\"action\": \"model\"}.\nYour program is checked at no cost: it is run from the first board of every level of this game you have seen, a\ndemonstration's or your own, through every action played on it, and must give every board the game showed and say won\nexactly where the level completed (and lost, if you define it, where the game ended). A failed check is reported to\nyou; after 3 failed checks in an instance, a program is used without a check. The benchmark then searches\nyour program for the shortest sequence of actions that wins from the board now, and plays it, stopping as soon as the\ngame shows a board your program did not predict, which is reported to you. You may send at most 6\nprograms per instance. A program that passes its check is kept for the game: when the game returns, its plan is played\nfirst, before you are asked.\n\nDemonstrations of game task-f2ec413b that you received before, shown again at no cost:\n\nEarlier demonstration 1: the benchmark's solver playing another fresh level of this game to its end, in 9 actions.\nIts first board:\n5555444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444\n5555444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444\n5555444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444\n5555444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444\n5555444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444444\n5555444444444444444333333333333333333334444444444444444444444444\n5555444444444444444333333333333033333334444444444444444444444444\n5555444444444444444333333333331003333334444444444444444444444444\n5555444444444444444333333333333133333334444444444444444444444444\n5555444444444444444333333333333333333334444444444444444444444444\n5555444444444444444333333333344444444444444444444444444444444444\n5555444444444444444333333333344444444444444444444444444444444444\n55554444444444444443333333333444444444444444